{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0a1604e2-ce16-4bb4-be76-a30782cd3bee","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9713a8e004383a83","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-kgcu36k6/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E002.md","content":"# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA in odh-model-controller\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the downstream authentication context that KEDA Prometheus-type triggers need on\nOpenShift for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA autoscaling. On OpenShift, KEDA ScaledObjects\ncannot query Thanos/Prometheus without an authenticated context; a parallel reconciler flow in\nodh-model-controller creates that context for LLMInferenceService resources using standalone KEDA,\nfollowing the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern at\n`internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go` that already serves v1beta1\nInferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement a reconciler flow in odh-model-controller that, for LLMInferenceService resources\n  configured with standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers, creates the same per-namespace\n  authentication context the existing reconciler creates for v1beta1 InferenceService:\n  - a dedicated ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth`),\n  - a ServiceAccountToken Secret (`kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type),\n  - a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access,\n  - a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers.\n- Follow the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` as the reference implementation for resource naming,\n  owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup (architecture context: odh-model-controller.md).\n- Determine (as part of implementation) whether upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 requires downstream\n  adaptation beyond the authentication context — e.g., RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift — and\n  incorporate any such adjustments (open question owned by the KServe team; resolved during\n  implementation).\n- Documentation-facing behavior: operators reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in their KEDA\n  trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift (the documentation\n  epic covers the operator-facing instructions).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` configured with a Prometheus-type trigger,\n  when odh-model-controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication context exists:\n  ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR\n  supplying bearer token and CA cert.\n- The KEDA Prometheus trigger successfully queries Thanos/Prometheus using the TriggerAuthentication\n  context over TLS 1.2+ (measured by a ScaledObject becoming active and reporting metrics from an\n  authenticated query).\n- Resource naming, owner references, and lifecycle cleanup follow the existing\n  `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern: authentication resources are owned by the LLMInferenceService and\n  cleaned up when it is deleted or the KEDA configuration is removed.\n- Resources are only created for LLMInferenceService resources that actually configure standalone\n  KEDA (no resources created for static or WVA-mediated deployments).\n- The downstream adaptation question (whether kserve/kserve#5839 requires changes beyond the\n  authentication context, such as RBAC or TLS adjustments) is answered and any required adaptations\n  are implemented in this reconciler flow.\n- Unit and integration tests cover the reconciler for the LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA case,\n  including no-op behavior for non-KEDA deployments.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR1 [P0] (partial): LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined\n  triggers without requiring WVA deployment — this epic makes Prometheus-type triggers (the primary\n  supported standalone KEDA trigger source for inference metrics on OpenShift) work with an\n  authenticated context.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nDepends on the standalone KEDA API fields (`spec.scaling.keda`) being available in the\nLLMInferenceService spec delivered by the llmisvc-controller work — the reconciler builds against\nthat API surface.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":43192,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a848e2b309a71f2","agent_id":"ab6a67c1bf6c03009","session_id":"41f0b542-dbb8-4d5e-a349-930fe2b66c78","uuid":"5406a47c-b6b8-41df-8f4c-87947ebebff0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:15:36.498Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
