{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-994950b9-1229-4b3f-8fe7-9b96647fd915","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f655c2e222d30b8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-kgcu36k6/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E003.md","content":"# LLMInferenceService autoscaling documentation: decision guide, recipes, troubleshooting\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the documentation for LLMInferenceService autoscaling, covering both autoscaling\npaths (WVA-mediated and standalone KEDA) and static allocation. It provides a decision guide for\nchoosing between the paths, configuration recipes per workload shape, troubleshooting guidance, a\nsupported configuration matrix, and the prerequisite/operator guidance needed to run standalone KEDA\non OpenShift (KEDA installation, TriggerAuthentication for Prometheus queries).\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Decision guide**: use-case-based recommendations for WVA-mediated autoscaling vs. standalone KEDA\n  vs. static allocation (mitigates the \"two scaling paths in a single CRD may confuse operators\"\n  risk; CEL validation error messages complement it).\n- **Configuration recipes per workload shape**, including the RFE's example scenario (a coding\n  assistant deployment that scales prefill and decode pools during business hours and releases GPUs\n  overnight).\n- **Scale-to-zero and scale-from-zero guidance**: KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` configuration,\n  recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control queue metrics, and\n  example ScaledObject templates (mitigates the standalone KEDA scale-from-zero reliability risk).\n- **Realistic cold start expectations** for scale-from-zero latency, referencing the fast model\n  startup RFEs (RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139) and headroom configuration for\n  latency-sensitive workloads.\n- **KEDA prerequisite documentation**: RHOAI does not auto-install KEDA (platform policy overlay\n  0008); document KEDA as a prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository\n  (ODH-ADR-0007).\n- **Authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift**: instructions for operators to reference\n  the TriggerAuthentication resource in their KEDA trigger definitions.\n- **Disaggregated scaling guidance**: independent prefill/decode scaling configuration and the\n  same-path (actuator consistency) requirement.\n- **Supported configuration matrix** and **troubleshooting paths** (including behavior when\n  Prometheus metrics become unavailable — replica count holds steady).\n- **Observability guidance**: where to see scaling decision events (KEDA ScaledObject events for the\n  standalone path; WVA events for the WVA path) and ScaledObject metrics for dashboard integration.\n- **Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments** (e.g., polling-interval impact at\n  large GPU fleet scale) — incorporating input from the Performance/PSAP team once available.\n- **Scale-from-zero latency targets**: incorporate the p50/p95 target (the RFE telemetry section\n  references a 60-second p50 threshold) and resulting headroom pre-scaling recommendations, based on\n  input from the llm-d team once available.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A published decision guide covers WVA-mediated autoscaling, standalone KEDA, and static allocation\n  with use-case-based recommendations.\n- Configuration recipes exist for both paths, including scale-to-zero, scale-from-zero (with\n  recommended EPP queue metric trigger configurations and example ScaledObject templates), and\n  disaggregated prefill/decode scaling.\n- KEDA is documented as a prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository\n  (RHOAI does not auto-install KEDA).\n- Operators are instructed how to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated\n  Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n- Troubleshooting content covers common failure modes (misconfigured triggers failing the\n  zero-to-one transition, Prometheus metric outages holding replica count) and a supported\n  configuration matrix is published.\n- Scale-from-zero latency expectations and headroom recommendations incorporate the agreed p50/p95\n  target from the llm-d team (open question; content updated or a revision pass scheduled once the\n  target lands).\n- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments incorporates Performance/PSAP team input\n  (open question; content updated or a revision pass scheduled once available).\n- All content is technically reviewed against the implemented behavior of both autoscaling paths and\n  the OpenShift authentication flow (mandatory technical review).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR7 [P1]: Well-lit path documentation covers both autoscaling paths with decision guide,\n  configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting\n- HLR4 [P0] (documentation portion): scale-from-zero guidance for the standalone path — recommended\n  trigger configurations using EPP queue metrics and example ScaledObject templates (risk mitigation\n  for standalone KEDA scale-from-zero reliability)\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":44198,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a848e2b309a71f2","agent_id":"ab6a67c1bf6c03009","session_id":"41f0b542-dbb8-4d5e-a349-930fe2b66c78","uuid":"bcbd1491-7413-4bec-8938-5c96750b5544","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:15:55.003Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
