{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-941692b1ca2aff3f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E002\n3\ttitle: KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA\n4\t  in odh-model-controller\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n6\tcomponent: Serving Orchestration\n7\tteam: Serving team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n12\tai_signals:\n13\t  change_specificity: 1\n14\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n15\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n16\t  existing_foundation: 1\n17\t  open_questions: -1\n18\t  external_dependency: 0\n19\t  human_process_gates: 0\n20\t  repo_access: 1\n21\t  architecture_claims: 1\n22\timplementation_type: null\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 5\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA in odh-model-controller\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tThis epic delivers the downstream authentication context that KEDA Prometheus-type triggers need on\n36\tOpenShift for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA autoscaling. On OpenShift, KEDA ScaledObjects\n37\tcannot query Thanos/Prometheus without an authenticated context; a parallel reconciler flow in\n38\todh-model-controller creates that context for LLMInferenceService resources using standalone KEDA,\n39\tfollowing the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern at\n40\t`internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go` that already serves v1beta1\n41\tInferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Implement a reconciler flow in odh-model-controller that, for LLMInferenceService resources\n46\t  configured with standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers, creates the same per-namespace\n47\t  authentication context the existing reconciler creates for v1beta1 InferenceService:\n48\t  - a dedicated ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth`),\n49\t  - a ServiceAccountToken Secret (`kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type),\n50\t  - a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access,\n51\t  - a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers.\n52\t- Follow the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` as the reference implementation for resource naming,\n53\t  owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup (architecture context: odh-model-controller.md).\n54\t- Determine (as part of implementation) whether upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 requires downstream\n55\t  adaptation beyond the authentication context — e.g., RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift — and\n56\t  incorporate any such adjustments (open question owned by the KServe team; resolved during\n57\t  implementation).\n58\t- Documentation-facing behavior: operators reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in their KEDA\n59\t  trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift (the documentation\n60\t  epic covers the operator-facing instructions).\n61\t\n62\t## Acceptance Criteria\n63\t\n64\t- Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` configured with a Prometheus-type trigger,\n65\t  when odh-model-controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication context exists:\n66\t  ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR\n67\t  supplying bearer token and CA cert.\n68\t- The KEDA Prometheus trigger successfully queries Thanos/Prometheus using the TriggerAuthentication\n69\t  context over TLS 1.2+ (measured by a ScaledObject becoming active and reporting metrics from an\n70\t  authenticated query).\n71\t- Resource naming, owner references, and lifecycle cleanup follow the existing\n72\t  `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern: authentication resources are owned by the LLMInferenceService and\n73\t  cleaned up when it is deleted or the KEDA configuration is removed.\n74\t- Resources are only created for LLMInferenceService resources that actually configure standalone\n75\t  KEDA (no resources created for static or WVA-mediated deployments).\n76\t- The downstream adaptation question (whether kserve/kserve#5839 requires changes beyond the\n77\t  authentication context, such as RBAC or TLS adjustments) is answered and any required adaptations\n78\t  are implemented in this reconciler flow.\n79\t- Unit and integration tests cover the reconciler for the LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA case,\n80\t  including no-op behavior for non-KEDA deployments.\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability\n83\t\n84\t- HLR1 [P0] (partial): LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined\n85\t  triggers without requiring WVA deployment — this epic makes Prometheus-type triggers (the primary\n86\t  supported standalone KEDA trigger source for inference metrics on OpenShift) work with an\n87\t  authenticated context.\n88\t\n89\t## Dependencies\n90\t\n91\tDepends on the standalone KEDA API fields (`spec.scaling.keda`) being available in the\n92\tLLMInferenceService spec delivered by the llmisvc-controller work — the reconciler builds against\n93\tthat API surface.\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a56a4f290a28a50c","agent_id":"aa4d7dee2893ef04c","session_id":"41f0b542-dbb8-4d5e-a349-930fe2b66c78","uuid":"ede3d83b-bb08-4654-9a15-2a163665fd44","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:18:10.733Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-941692b1ca2aff3f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
