{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8afa92ea21e42a85","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8008\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'E001 and E002 score human_process_gates: 0 while every strategy-level\n10\t    process gate is Pending human review (decomposition Health Warning 1 says human\n11\t    verification is required before implementation starts), yet E003 scores -1 partly\n12\t    citing the same pending Documentation Support gate. Apply one consistent treatment:\n13\t    either the pending strategy gates justify -1 for all implementation epics, or\n14\t    the E003 -1 should rest solely on the docs technical-review sign-off.'\n15\terror: null\n16\t---\n17\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8008\n18\t\n19\t## Review Summary\n20\t\n21\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n22\tRecommendation: accept\n23\t\n24\tFull decomposition (triage: null, epic_count 3 — consistent). The decomposition cleanly\n25\ttranslates the strategy into three epics along component boundaries (kserve llmisvc-controller,\n26\todh-model-controller, Documentation), with a correct 3-node DAG, complete HLR traceability,\n27\tand well-dispositioned unknowns (no Investigation epic needed — every open question maps to an\n28\tAC, a docs-content input, or a documented fallback).\n29\t\n30\t## Criterion Details\n31\t\n32\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n33\tAll 7 HLRs map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix in the decomposition summary is\n34\tcomplete and matches the per-epic \"HLR Traceability\" sections (E001: HLR1-6; E002: HLR1 partial;\n35\tE003: HLR7 + HLR4 documentation portion). Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (blocks all P0\n36\twork) is P0, and E002 inherits P0 with a stated justification (prerequisite to HLR1 being usable\n37\ton OpenShift). E003 is a docs-authoring epic — exempt from priority inheritance; its P0 derives\n38\tfrom the strategy's `priority: Critical` per the mandated mapping, and the summary explicitly\n39\tdocuments this so planners don't misread it. HLR6 (P1) is bundled into E001 rather than split,\n40\twith a valid justification: scaling status conditions and event attribution share the same\n41\timplementation surface (`scaling.go` branching) as the P0 controller work — incidental polish on\n42\tthe P0 change, not a distinct deferrable feature, so no priority collapse.\n43\t\n44\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n45\tNo circular dependencies. Frontmatter `dependencies` (E001: [], E002: [E001], E003:\n46\t[E001, E002]) exactly match the summary diagram, and arrows run dependency → dependent per the\n47\t`graph TD` convention. The E001 → E002 edge is a legitimate Rule 13 build-against dependency:\n48\tE002's reconciler code builds against the `spec.scaling.keda` API surface/CRD shape that E001\n49\tproduces — a schema artifact, not an image digest/endpoint configuration reference, so a\n50\t`dependencies` edge (not merely an AC gate) is correct. E001 → E003 and E002 → E003 are valid\n51\tRule 11 docs-last edges. No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics\n52\texist (both repos are in-platform), so no forbidden parallel-pair edges and no Rule 25 AC\n53\trequirement. Critical path length 3 matches the M-standard heuristic (3-4). No data/artifact\n54\tdependency is missing from the DAG: E003's operator documentation of the TriggerAuthentication\n55\tflow is covered by the E002 → E003 edge. No conditional branch files exist, and no main-plan\n56\tepic references branch nodes.\n57\t\n58\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n59\tEach component/team tuple is a separate epic: E001 (Serving Orchestration / Serving team, kserve\n60\trepo), E002 (Serving Orchestration / Serving team, odh-model-controller repo), E003\n61\t(Documentation / Documentation team). The same-team E001/E002 pair is correctly split by\n62\tcomponent/repo. No scope duplication: E002 defers operator-facing instructions to E003 rather\n63\tthan re-authoring them, and no epic rebuilds a structure another epic owns. No work-product type\n64\tmixing — E003 is pure content authoring. Epic sizes are proportionate to the M estimate\n65\t(2-3 sprints total across 3 epics); E001 is at the upper bound (~1-2 sprints per the strategy's\n66\town effort estimate) but within the ~2-week epic limit and reflects the strategy treating the\n67\tllmisvc-controller change as one coupled work stream (API + validation + controller branching\n68\tcannot be delivered partially).\n69\t\n70\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n71\tAll three epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (controller code + API\n72\tsurface; reconciler + auth resources; published documentation). No Investigation epic was\n73\tcreated, and the investigation scan's dispositions hold up under the \"does the answer change\n74\tdownstream structure?\" test: the upstream-PR adaptation question expands E002's scope at most\n75\t(fix-and-retry within the same epic); the p50/p95 and Prometheus-capacity questions are docs\n76\tcontent inputs (ACs on E003); the two \"needs validation\" assumptions are validation ACs on E001;\n77\tthe upstream timing risk has a viable strategy-documented fallback (Rule 21 → AC, not epic). No\n78\t`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, which is correct given no gates.\n79\t\n80\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n81\tSignal fields are type-appropriate (ai_signals on all three Implementation epics;\n82\tinvestigation_signals null). Rationales in the ai-signals files are detailed and match the\n83\tfrontmatter values, and cross-epic treatment of the upstream PR is consistent (E001\n84\texternal_dependency: -1 for merge timing; E002 open_questions: -1 for the adaptation question it\n85\towns). One arguable inconsistency: E001 and E002 score `human_process_gates: 0` (\"no approval\n86\tstep beyond normal code review\") even though every strategy-level process gate (Architecture\n87\tReview, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) is \"Pending human review\" and the\n88\tdecomposition's own Health Warning 1 states human verification is required before implementation\n89\tstarts — while E003 scores -1 partly citing the same pending Documentation Support gate. Either\n90\tthe pending strategy gates block all epics (all should be -1) or they are uniformly out of scope\n91\t(E003's -1 should rest on the docs technical-review sign-off alone). Minor: does not change the\n92\tHigh ratings or the epic ordering.\n93\t\n94\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n95\tAll three epics have testable, strategy-derived ACs. E001 carries all seven strategy acceptance\n96\tcriteria plus validation-of-assumption ACs (idleReplicaCount pause/resume, EPP metrics at zero\n97\treplicas) and the upstream fallback AC. E002's ACs are concrete and measurable (ScaledObject\n98\tactive via authenticated TLS 1.2+ query, ownership/cleanup semantics, no-op for non-KEDA\n99\tdeployments). E003 (docs-authoring) has the rule-mandated technical-review AC. E001 is additive,\n100\tnot a replacement (existing WVA path preserved), and carries backwards-compatibility ACs, so no\n101\trollback/feature-flag AC is mandated. No konflux chain exists, so no build-pipeline-green AC is\n102\trequired. Epic bodies reference siblings only as \"the documentation epic\" — no draft-ID\n103\treferences.\n104\t\n105\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n106\tThe combined epic set covers the full strategy scope: both autoscaling paths, API/validation/\n107\tcontroller changes, downstream TriggerAuthentication flow, scale-to/from-zero, disaggregated\n108\tscaling, observability, and the complete docs scope (decision guide, recipes, troubleshooting,\n109\tsupported configuration matrix, KEDA prerequisite per overlay 0008/ODH-ADR-0007, capacity\n110\tguidance, latency targets). All four NFRs are carried (backwards compatibility → E001 ACs;\n111\tsecur