tation / Documentation team).\nE001 and E002 are correctly separate epics despite the same canonical component and team — different\nrepos, different logical changes (Rule 2 permits single-epic only for same component+team+same\nlogical change). Component mapping to the canonical list is logged with rationale in the summary.\nE003 correctly separates content authoring from code implementation (work-product type boundary).\n\nNo scope duplication: E001 implements the controller path, E002 the OpenShift auth context, E003 the\noperator-facing content; the TriggerAuthentication touchpoints are explicitly delegated rather than\nduplicated. No work-product type mixing within any epic (E003 is pure docs-authoring).\n\nSize: E001 bundles upstream landing/backport, API types, validation, controller branching, status\nconditions, and the test matrix — the strategy estimates the KServe portion at 1-2 sprints, the upper\nedge of the ~2-week guidance. Acceptable: it is one logical change in one repo (splitting it would\nviolate the same-logical-change boundary rule) and upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 supplies the\nimplementation. Recorded as a minor observation.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nNo Investigation epics; all three epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact\n(controller code + API, reconciler flow, published documentation). All `gated_by` fields are null and\nno epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies`, so no gating-metadata obligations are triggered.\n\nInvestigation scan dispositions are sound under the decision rule (\"does the answer change which\ndownstream epics exist or what they do?\"): the three open questions (upstream adaptation beyond auth\ncontext, p50/p95 scale-from-zero target, Prometheus capacity guidance) are ACs on the relevant\nimplementation epics — the RBAC/TLS possibility is already inside E002's scope and ACs, and the two\ndocs-input questions determine content, not epic structure. The \"needs validation\" assumptions are\ncorrectly treated as validation-of-assumption ACs (E001), and the upstream PR timing is correctly\nhandled per Rule 21 (viable carry-downstream fallback → AC, not a separate epic) with the tiered\ndelivery evaluation documented.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nAll three epics are type Implementation and carry the 9-signal `ai_signals` set (investigation_signals\nnull) — correct signal set per type. All three ai-signals rationale files exist, and every value in\neach file matches the frontmatter.\n\nSpot-checks against the rubric and strategy content:\n- E001 `external_dependency: -1` — upstream PR #5839 acceptance gates the work; justified.\n- E001 `open_questions: 0` — design fully specified by upstream #5646/#5839; residual items are\n  validation-of-assumption ACs, not approach-changing questions. Neutral is right.\n- E002 `open_questions: -1` — the open question (RBAC/TLS adaptation beyond auth context) sits inside\n  this epic's own scope and could expand its implementation; matches the -1 rubric condition even\n  though it doesn't change epic structure (different question).\n- E003 `adapter_pattern: 0` (N/A for content authoring) and `human_process_gates: -1` (mandatory\n  technical review) — both correct.\n- `architecture_claims: +1` on all three — verified: kserve.md, odh-model-controller.md,\n  workload-variant-autoscaler.md, llm-d.md, and PLATFORM.md all exist in\n  `.context/architecture-context/`.\n\nNo signal value contradicts the rubric; rationales are specific and match frontmatter values.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy: all seven strategy acceptance criteria appear\nin E001's AC list (mutual exclusivity rejection, no VariantAutoscaling CR, scale-to-zero, independent\nprefill/decode scaling, metric-outage stability, static-deployment no-op, trigger-driven scale-up),\nE002 adds reconciler-specific ACs (auth resource set, TLS 1.2+ query success, owner-ref cleanup,\nno-op for non-KEDA deployments), and E003 has content ACs including the two open-question inputs.\n\nRule-mandated ACs: E003 (docs-authoring) has the Rule 24 \"technically reviewed against\nimplementation\" AC. No konflux chain exists, so Rule 25 does not apply. The change is additive (WVA\npath preserved), not a replacement of an existing capability, so Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag is not\ntriggered — and backwards-compatibility ACs are present regardless.\n\nNo draft-ID references (\"E001\"/\"E003\") appear in any epic body — cross-epic relationships are\ndescribed in plain terms (\"the documentation epic\", \"the llmisvc-controller work\").\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: API/validation/controller changes (E001), downstream\nTriggerAuthentication flow (E002), and the full documentation surface — decision guide, per-workload\nrecipes, scale-to/from-zero guidance, cold-start expectations, KEDA prerequisite (overlay 0008 /\nODH-ADR-0007), TriggerAuthentication instructions, disaggregated guidance, configuration matrix,\ntroubleshooting, observability guidance (E003).\n\nNFRs: backwards compatibility → E001 ACs; observability → E001 status conditions + E003 guidance;\nsecurity → E002 TLS/TriggerAuthentication ACs; scalability → E003 capacity guidance AC plus health\nwarning (the strategy itself flags no measurable target). All five risks have carried-forward\nmitigations (upstream timing → E001 fallback AC; scale-from-zero reliability and cold-start latency\n→ E003 scope; KEDA dependency → E003 prerequisite docs; API complexity → E003 decision guide). All\nthree open questions are dispositioned with owning-epic ACs. Out-of-scope items are respected. A\ndocs-authoring epic exists (HLR7 / Documentation component), satisfying the Rule 11 documentation\nexpectation. No conditional branches are used and none are needed (no gated outcomes).\n\nMinor observation: E002 carries no explicit Risks/assumptions section even though its re